An issue in `test_lock_preemptible` in `tests/kernel/sched/schedule_api/src/test_sched_timeslice_and_lock.c` causes the test to fail: it calls `k_yield()` instead of `k_sleep(K_MSEC(100))` to make the current thread unready. Because `k_yield()` leaves the current thread (at priority `0`) in the ready state, the lower-priority worker thread (`tdata[2]` at priority `1`) is never scheduled. Replacing `k_yield()` with `k_sleep(K_MSEC(100))` as described by the test documentation makes the current thread unready so all worker threads can execute.

tests/kernel/sched/schedule_api/src/test_sched_timeslice_and_lock.c
<<<<<<<< SEARCH
	/* make current thread unready */
	k_yield();
	/* checkpoint: all other threads get executed */
========
	/* make current thread unready */
	k_sleep(K_MSEC(100));
	/* checkpoint: all other threads get executed */
>>>>>>>> REPLACE
